cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use or refer to SEACAST, please cite it as below."
type: software
title: "SEACAST: Segmented, Encoded, Availability-sampled broadCAST (specification)"
abstract: >-
  SEACAST (Segmented, Encoded, Availability-sampled broadCAST) is a protocol for
  getting large objects to many nodes of a peer-to-peer network fast. The source
  cuts an object into chunks and commits to them, and can compress it and add
  coded chunks. A node passes each chunk on as soon as it has checked it,
  without waiting for the whole object: it pushes chunks to some peers,
  announces them to others, and asks for those it misses. The same protocol
  serves broadcast, where every node needs the whole object, and sampled
  diffusion, where a node needs only part of it. It is being developed for
  Ethereum. This repository holds its specification, written as executable
  Python-like code, with a simulator that runs it, a Lean version of the
  specification, and proofs in Veil.
authors:
